Objective
Today I learned how to investigate active network connections on a Windows computer and trace a connection back to the process and Windows service responsible for it.
Commands Used
netstat -ano
Used to view active network connections, ports, connection states, and Process IDs (PIDs).
tasklist /fi "PID eq 4596"
Used to identify the process associated with PID 4596.
tasklist /svc /fi "PID eq 4596"
Used to identify the Windows service running under the process.
sc query DoSvc
Used to check the status of the Delivery Optimization service.
Investigation
I identified the following established TCP connection:
TCP    172.20.10.4:53692    196.49.32.6:80    ESTABLISHED    4596
Breakdown
Protocol: TCP
Local IP: 172.20.10.4
Local Port: 53692
Remote/Foreign IP: 196.49.32.6
Remote Port: 80
Connection State: ESTABLISHED
PID: 4596
I then investigated PID 4596 and found:
svchost.exe
I used tasklist /svc to investigate further and identified the service as:
DoSvc
DoSvc is the Delivery Optimization Windows service.
I then checked its status using sc query DoSvc and confirmed:
STATE : 4 RUNNING
Key Concepts Learned
Local IP
The local IP identifies my own computer/device involved in the connection.
Remote/Foreign IP
The remote or foreign IP identifies the other endpoint my computer is communicating with.
ESTABLISHED
This means an active TCP connection has been successfully established between the two endpoints.
PID
A Process ID identifies the specific process responsible for a network connection.
Investigation Workflow
The main workflow I learned today was:
Network Connection
        ↓
       PID
        ↓
     Process
        ↓
     Service
        ↓
   Service State
Security Lesson
A legitimate Windows process such as svchost.exe does not automatically mean that every network connection associated with it is safe.
As a security analyst, I should investigate the connection further by considering the remote IP, destination port, process, service, and whether the activity is expected.
What I Learned Today
I learned how to use Windows networking and process commands to investigate an active connection and identify the Windows service behind it. I also learned the difference between a local IP and a remote/foreign IP and how they are used during network investigations.
